      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=20783096",
      "title": "Comment on a Datadog pricing thread",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "customer",
      "published": "2019-08-23",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Had a similar bad experience. We had a situation where we were running instances with short durations; AWS bills by the second, but Datadog bills hourly. We ended up having a Datadog bill that was higher than our AWS bill! They had the opportunity to help us out - they ended up using it as leverage in their sales process, only willing to work with us IF we signed up for additional services. We tried to work with them, but eventually just ate the expense."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:3",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=35865745",
      "title": "Comment on an observability pricing thread",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "customer",
      "published": "2023-05-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Having recently dealt with a surprise runaway DataDog bill and personally completed lots of work to reduce our spend internally all I have to say is that their pricing is outrageously high and there is almost no way to put controls in place to prevent overspend."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:4",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46631061",
      "title": "Comment on an observability platform thread",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2026-01-15",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I can't get over how expensive these observability platforms are. Last I looked (and looked again just now), if we were to take all our structured logs from all services and send them to Datadog with our current retention policy, it would just about double our current IT spend. Instead, we use Grafana + Loki + ClickHouse and it's been mostly maintenance-free for years. Costs under $100/month. What am I missing? What's the real value that folks are getting out of these platforms?"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:5",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=38093281",
      "title": "Comment from a long-term Datadog customer on pricing model changes",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "customer",
      "published": "2023-11-01",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "As a long term DataDog customer... DataDog have an extractive pricing model. It's undergone a number of changes over the years that separated out features previously under the host price into their own products with separate monthly costs. They put limits on the numbers of unique containers you can have per host before charging container fees, so better hope you don't have too many crash-restart loops... Theres the per unique host per billing period pricing model that makes it impossible to scale your own infrastructure up and down to save on hardware costs, one personal case I can attest to was case the changes would halve the AWS bill but increase the DataDog bill by more than 10 times, a thousand percent."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:6",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45438010",
      "title": "Comment on service mesh adoption",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-10-01",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Ive seen it tried in a couple of places but didn't work super well; the last place couldn't because datadog apparently bills sidecar containers as additional hosts so using sidecar proxy would have doubled our datadog bill."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:7",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=25434387",
      "title": "Comment on managed metrics systems and cardinality",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2020-12-15",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Every managed metrics system will put a limit on cardinality because all mainstream available metrics systems cost more per cardinality to query and store. If they don't limit that you can assume you or some other customer is going to use up the clusters resources and cause an outage. Like most metrics systems, under the covers in Prometheus each unique combination of dimensions is the same as a new metric line."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:8",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41654011",
      "title": "Comment on making on-call sustainable",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-26",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Alert fatigue. Alert fatigue. Alert fatigue. It's the single biggest quality of life thing that you can do to help with the annoyance that is on call. If you know you're in store for the same alert again and again, or perhaps even know that you know you're going to get paged, it's hard to think about anything else. It becomes then a game of normalizing deviance and burnout: \"oh, we just ignored that one last time\". Ok, why are they alerts then if they can be ignored? It's just going to murder people's spirit after a while."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:9",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=32165415",
      "title": "Comment on on-call burnout",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2022-07-20",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Yeah I have a vivid memory of hysterically crying after getting less than 12 hours of sleep over 3 or 4 days during a particularly bad shift. My pager went off 30 minutes before the end of my shift and I lost it. I should've passed the pager but I was too \"up\" to recognize it and my manager at the time was completely clueless about oncall work. Actually at that same company getting paged 8-10 times off-hours during an oncall shift was not unheard of, the burnout was real... I'm surprised there aren't more lawsuits regarding this because the toll of sleep deprivation on health is well known."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:10",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48091591",
      "title": "Comment on living with a self-hosted Grafana/Loki/Mimir stack",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2026-05-11",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "The UX is dreadful? I could spend hours picking apart the terrible design decisions. After many years with the comparative luxury of the stackdriver tools on GCP, I moved to a BigCorp with a grafana/loki/mimir system and a whole devops army to maintain it. For two years I've been unable to find anything positive to say about this experience. Our devops folks are super smart, so I can only conclude that the software sucks."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:11",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48120391",
      "title": "Comment on self-hosting Grafana, Loki, Prometheus and Alloy for a small project",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2026-05-13",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I have tried to self host grafana (loki prom and alloy) as o11y stack for prepbook.app. This is hard. I have a bsc in cs not that it says something. I managed to do it eventually, after some research. It was not plug and play in any way. The docs kept saying this solution is not production ready even. I couldn't find the production guide, only the \"forget about self hosting and simply pay for us hosting this\". After I deployed it the UX was so abrasive my partner won't even try to go into it to figure out a problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:12",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48090579",
      "title": "Reply to a team that replaced its managed stack with Prometheus, Grafana and Loki",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2026-05-11",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "But now you need staffing/headcount to be experts in, and maintain, upgrade and be oncall for this stuff?"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:13",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48121224",
      "title": "Comment on scaling Prometheus and Thanos versus paying a vendor",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2026-05-13",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "There are ways to scale Prometheus (look at Thanos), but none of the solutions is really bug free... Prometheus, Thanos and even OpenTelemetry are full of those kind of problems - but at the same time it's the best we have and we should be grateful they're free and open source. I'd still choose an open source stack (and contribute to it) rather than go for a proprietary solution - we've all seen what happens with DataDog & co... at the same time DataDog / Splunk prices are simply unjustified. It's ironically cheaper to build a team of engineers that will maintain a sane observability stack instead of feeding the monster(s)."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:14",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40683695",
      "title": "Comment on OpenTelemetry and observability vendor lock-in",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-06-14",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "vendor lock-in with closed-source observability platforms is a significant challenge, especially for large organizations. When you instrument hundreds or thousands of applications with a specific tool, like the Datadog Agent, disentangling from that tool becomes nearly impossible without a massive investment of engineering time. In the Platform Engineering professional services space, we see this problem frequently. Enterprises are growing tired of big observability platform lock-in, especially when it comes to Datadog's opaque nature of your spend on their products, for example."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:15",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=44346010",
      "title": "Comment on enterprise logging incentives and FinOps",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-06-22",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "If you work for a large enterprise, there are so many dev teams supporting so many products that \"we haven't figured out what's actually worth logging\" is just disconnected from the developer incentives in those teams (ship features fast, fix your problems even faster because nobody has time for that BS) as well as ops incentives (the servers ARE on fire, and the devs didn't log enough). FinOps comes last, if there's even cost tracking per team in the observability suite. You don't understand why DataDog has a $44 billion market cap. It's yet another instance of Finance complaining that the transition to The Cloud gave every engineer a corporate credit card with no spend controls or a way for Finance to turn off the spigot."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:16",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42255013",
      "title": "Comment on a 'we ditched Kubernetes' post, from someone running 20+ clusters",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-11-27",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I have been working with Kubernetes since 2016 and keep being pragmatic on tech stuff. Currently support 20+ clusters with a team of 5 people across 2 clouds plus on-prem... \"147 false positive alerts\" - alert and monitoring hygiene helps. Anything will have a low signal-to-noise ratio if not properly taken care of. Been there, done that... \"5 different monitoring tools / 3 separate logging solutions\" - should be at most one for each case. 3 different cloud providers? So come up with a cloud-agnostic solution."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:17",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=37828173",
      "title": "Comment on logging controls in regulated organizations",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2023-10-10",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "In my experience there are much more stringent control over logging. Tokenization is the default for anything related to PII or customer data. Log retention rarely goes over 30 days. Data residency is now in the conversation when talking about storage. Maybe smaller companies can fly under the radar, but any larger companies with auditing and compliance controls take it seriously."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:18",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49214950",
      "title": "Show HN post framing the observability market's two bad options",
      "venue": "Hacker News",
      "speaker": "founder",
      "published": "2026-08-07",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "There are a lot of big players in the observability platform world. DataDog comes to mind, and they are EXPENSIVE. I've seen DataDog bills that cost more than the infrastructure they are monitoring!... They all share a few things in common: They are essentially either silo'ed stores locked behind some vendor system with ever increasing bills, or they require a lot of resources to operate yourself."
    },
